{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"central","type":"text","text":"## CENTRAL BRAIN\n\nThe one vault you actually live in.\n\nHolds raw thinking, planning, journal, people CRM, research, internal strategy, and every brand subfolder that feeds a satellite.\n\nIf a satellite disagrees with the central brain, the central brain wins. Everything else is downstream.","x":-440,"y":-1080,"width":880,"height":300,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"exclude","type":"text","text":"## UNIVERSAL EXCLUSIONS\n\nNever auto-mirror to any satellite. Preview and ask, or keep central-only.\n\n- Founder-private notes, journal, reflection\n- Vault-management material itself\n- Raw research and unverified clippings\n- Meeting notes with confidential counterparty material\n- Competitive analysis, pricing internals, revenue and pipeline data\n- Risk registers, governance internals\n- Live customer, lead, or partner names\n- Anything tagged internal-only, partner-confidential, pre-disclosure\n- Notes flagged `mutability: review-first` without explicit approval","x":-2360,"y":-100,"width":760,"height":660,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"patternD","type":"text","text":"## PATTERN D\n### Mixed-authority agent bridge\n\n**Use when:** an agent fleet needs curated context and a bounded place to work without access to the private vault or canonical CRM.\n\n**Default:** public-safe context is published out; writes are limited to registered workspace mounts and quarantined proposals.","x":880,"y":-440,"width":640,"height":520,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"patternD-mech","type":"text","text":"### Pattern D mechanics\n\n1. `Context/public-safe/`: central publisher writes, agents read\n2. `Workspace/<mount>/`: registered agents write bounded work artefacts\n3. `CRM/Proposals/`: agents append proposals; reviewer or importer decides\n4. Accepted outputs return through Pattern E\n5. Missing authority metadata means no write","x":880,"y":140,"width":640,"height":420,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"patternC","type":"text","text":"## PATTERN C\n### Stripped mirror\n\n**Use when:** the satellite is one step from public. Content production repo, near-publish surface, partner-facing workspace.\n\n**Default:** every write runs through a stripping pass.\n\n**Typical scope:**\n- Blog drafts, email copy, landing pages\n- Tutorials and use cases\n- Public methodology and identity\n- Anything bound for an external CMS","x":120,"y":-440,"width":640,"height":520,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"patternC-mech","type":"text","text":"### Pattern C mechanics\n\n1. Resolve destination\n2. Run stripping pass\n3. If the note cannot be cleanly stripped without gutting it, do not mirror. Flag for deliberate authoring instead.\n4. Write the stripped version\n5. Show what was removed so the human can verify the cut","x":120,"y":140,"width":640,"height":420,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"patternA","type":"text","text":"## PATTERN A\n### Full mirror\n\n**Use when:** the satellite is a structural twin. Same shape as the main vault. Dogfood or demo operating vault.\n\n**Default:** straight copy with slug adjustment.\n\n**Typical scope:**\n- Marketing content and drafts\n- Offers and public positioning\n- Methodology and process docs\n- Project briefs and plans\n- Publish logs and registers","x":-1400,"y":-440,"width":640,"height":520,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"patternA-mech","type":"text","text":"### Pattern A mechanics\n\n1. Resolve mirror path in satellite\n2. Copy note with frontmatter preserved\n3. Rewrite internal wikilinks where the target exists in the satellite\n4. Drop links where it does not\n5. Report both paths\n6. Same note, two homes, one source of truth","x":-1400,"y":140,"width":640,"height":420,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"patternB","type":"text","text":"## PATTERN B\n### Narrow mirror\n\n**Use when:** the satellite is a dedicated public brain with its own structure. Different folder shape, different audience.\n\n**Default:** public-facing material mirrors freely. Internal strategy stays put.\n\n**Typical scope:**\n- Narrative, brand, voice\n- Go-to-market collateral\n- Public product or protocol explainers\n- Tutorials and explainer content","x":-640,"y":-440,"width":640,"height":520,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"patternB-mech","type":"text","text":"### Pattern B mechanics\n\n1. Decide if the note is in the public-facing slice\n2. If yes, resolve mapped destination in the satellite's structure\n3. Mirror with slug adjustment\n4. If the note touches internal strategy, preview both paths and ask\n5. Keep the satellite's folder shape, not the main vault's","x":-640,"y":140,"width":640,"height":420,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"payoff","type":"text","text":"## THE PAYOFF\n\nOne writing surface. Many downstream brains.\n\nYou stop choosing where a note lives. You write it in the right brand folder once, and the system propagates it to every place it needs to be, in the right shape, with the right things stripped out.\n\nFiling becomes infrastructure. Your attention goes back to the work.","x":220,"y":720,"width":820,"height":520,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"agent","type":"text","text":"## AGENT BEHAVIOUR\n\nWhat any coding agent does on every write inside a brand-scoped folder:\n\n1. Detect which brand the note belongs to\n2. Resolve the equivalent destination in the satellite\n3. If the destination folder is missing, pause and propose, do not create silently\n4. Run the pattern-specific transform (copy, slug, strip)\n5. Write to both locations\n6. Report both paths in the response\n7. If the content is in an excluded zone, preview and ask first","x":-760,"y":720,"width":880,"height":520,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"strip","type":"text","text":"## THE STRIPPING PASS\n\nApplies to any pattern that crosses a sensitivity boundary.\n\n**Remove on the way out:**\n- Customer, lead, partner, counterparty names\n- Meeting attendees, internal dates, internal commentary\n- Revenue, pricing, deal-size, runway, pipeline\n- Competitive intel and internal vs-competitor framing\n- Personnel commentary, 1-1 context\n- Internal-only wikilinks\n- Internal frontmatter (owners, reviewers, mutability)\n- TODOs and open questions not fit for the destination surface\n\n**Preserve:**\n- The public claim\n- The voice and structure\n- Public citations\n- Destination-repo frontmatter conventions\n\n**Safety valve:** if stripping guts the note, do not mirror. Author deliberately in the destination instead.","x":1660,"y":-540,"width":760,"height":1100,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"register","type":"text","text":"## ADDING A NEW BRAIN\n\nOne config row, one entry-point note, one canvas update.\n\n1. Add a row to the satellite register in `AGENTS.md`\n2. Pick a pattern (A / B / C / D)\n3. Write a brand-specific entry-point note that documents the strip/preserve list and any scope quirks\n4. Update the human-facing map and this canvas\n5. Run a dry-run mirror on one note before turning the default on\n\nA new brand should take an afternoon to wire up, not a quarter.","x":1660,"y":720,"width":760,"height":520,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"overrides","type":"text","text":"## OVERRIDE COMMANDS\n\nWhen the default routing is wrong, the human stays in charge.\n\n- `main only` — skip the satellite copy\n- `satellite only` — write to a satellite without filing centrally (use sparingly)\n- `parallel file this` — explicit mirror of an existing note\n- `preview` — show both destinations before writing anything\n\nOverrides are spoken in chat. The agent confirms before acting.","x":-1980,"y":740,"width":760,"height":500,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"why","type":"text","text":"## WHY CODIFY\n\nWithout codified rules:\n- Same note gets duplicated by hand\n- Versions drift\n- Sensitive content leaks into public repos\n- You spend mental energy filing instead of thinking\n\nWith codified rules:\n- Filing is a tool call, not a decision\n- Stripping happens automatically\n- New brands plug in with a config row\n- The agent reports both destinations so you can verify","x":840,"y":-1480,"width":820,"height":500,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"contract","type":"text","text":"## OPERATING CONTRACT\n\nA single `AGENTS.md` (or equivalent) at the vault root.\n\nDefines:\n- Registered satellites and their roots\n- Which pattern each satellite uses (A / B / C / D)\n- Universal exclusions\n- Override commands\n- Agent behaviour when filing is ambiguous\n\nEvery coding agent reads this on session start. The rules live in the repo, not in your memory.","x":-1630,"y":-1480,"width":820,"height":500,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"title","type":"text","text":"# Multi-Brain Management System\n\nA generalised pattern for running one source-of-truth vault that auto-files into any number of satellite brains.\n\nWhat is possible when sync and filing rules are codified into the operating contract, not held in your head.","x":-440,"y":-1800,"width":880,"height":480,"color":"1"}
	],
	"edges":[
